<p class="heritage-longread__dek">A short companion to the Rycroft story. The Steinkes reach this family through <strong>Bette Doreen Steinke</strong>, who married Sam Rycroft in 1959. This page goes as far back as the record now honestly allows &mdash; to Bette&rsquo;s parents &mdash; and names, at the end, the chases that would carry the line across the ocean to the German village it came from.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-durchhalten"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Prologue</span>Durchhalten</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Sexsmith, Alberta · German Lutheran prairie</p><p>Most of the families in this larger story crossed an ocean and left a paper trail behind them &mdash; a parish register, a passenger manifest, a homestead file. The Steinkes crossed one too, almost certainly; but for now the trail runs out at the edge of the Sexsmith prairie, with a German Lutheran farmer named Henry and his wife Martha, and the daughter they raised who married into the Rycroft line.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Durchhalten &mdash; to hold out.</p></aside><p>There is a German word for what carried families like theirs: <em>durchhalten</em> &mdash; <em>to hold out</em>, to endure, to keep your grip on a thing through a long hard stretch when letting go would be easier. It is the word for a Pomeranian or Volhynian-German farm family that pulled up its roots in eastern Europe, crossed to the Canadian plains among strangers, and held a corner of cold prairie until it became home. The Steinkes did that. We do not yet know the name of the village they held out <em>from</em> &mdash; only that they held, and that a daughter of theirs, born at Sexsmith in the middle of the Second World War, carried the holding forward into another family entirely.</p><p>This is the short, honest record of as much of them as the documents have so far given up.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-henry-martha-steinke-the-sexsmith-homestead"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter One</span>Henry &amp; Martha Steinke &mdash; the Sexsmith homestead</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Henry &amp; Martha Steinke · the Sexsmith homestead</p><p>The Steinke line in this family reaches its documented floor with <strong>Henry Steinke</strong> and his wife <strong>Martha</strong> &mdash; a German farming couple of the <strong>Sexsmith</strong> district in the Peace Country of northwestern Alberta, the parents of Bette Doreen Steinke. They were, on every available sign, part of one of the great migrations that built the Canadian prairies: the <strong>German-speaking settlers</strong> who poured onto the plains between the 1890s and the 1920s, many of them <strong>Lutheran</strong>, a great number of them coming not directly from Germany but from the German colonies scattered across the Russian Empire &mdash; the Volga, the Black Sea, Volhynia &mdash; or from the eastern marches of Prussia and Pomerania, where the surname <em>Steinke</em> is thickest on the ground.</p><p>The Peace Country sat at the far end of that wave. Its land opened late &mdash; the railway only reached Sexsmith around 1916 &mdash; so many of its German families were <strong>second-stage migrants</strong>, people who had first homesteaded farther south, in Saskatchewan or Manitoba or the Dakotas, and then pushed north to the Peace when the older districts filled. Whether the Steinkes followed that southern road or came straight to the Peace is one of the things still to be learned. What is certain is the religious and cultural ground they stood on: this was Lutheran prairie. A cluster of Steinkes lies in the <strong>Emmaus Lutheran Cemetery at Teepee Creek</strong>, the German Protestant burying-ground of that country &mdash; the kind of place a family like this kept faith, married, and was finally laid down.</p><p>Henry and Martha raised a family on that prairie, and one of their children carried the name out of the German-Lutheran world and into the Rycrofts.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-bette-doreen-steinke-1942-2025-into-the-rycroft-"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Two</span>Bette Doreen Steinke (1942&ndash;2025) &mdash; into the Rycroft line</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1942&ndash;2025 · Bette Steinke, into the Rycroft line</p><p><strong>Bette Doreen Steinke</strong> was born at <strong>Sexsmith on 26 April 1942</strong> &mdash; the <strong>fourth child</strong> of Henry and Martha, with at least three older siblings ahead of her and sisters who would outlive her. She came of age in the post-war Peace Country, and on <strong>30 October 1959</strong>, at Sexsmith, she married <strong>Samuel Eric John &ldquo;Sam&rdquo; Rycroft</strong> &mdash; the grandson, though neither of them is likely to have dwelt on it, of a man born under a Hawaiian sky. A German Lutheran farmer&rsquo;s daughter and the descendant of a Honolulu soda-maker, married in a small prairie town: it is exactly the kind of meeting the Canadian west was built to make.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>Not a name in an archive, but Nana Bette &mdash; remembered.</p></aside><div class="heritage-longread__ribbon" aria-hidden="true"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Henry &amp; Martha Steinke</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">Sexsmith</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Bette</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1942&ndash;2025</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Lana</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1961&ndash;2015</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Melanie</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1983</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Patience · Daniel · Faith</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">2013&ndash;2017</span></span></div><p>Sam and Bette raised four children &mdash; <strong>Lorne, Lana, Vance,</strong> and <strong>Clark</strong> &mdash; in the Grande Prairie country. Through their daughter <strong>Lana Lyn Rycroft</strong> the Steinke line runs down to <strong>Melanie</strong>, and on to <strong>Patience, Daniel, and Faith</strong> &mdash; so that a strain of German-Lutheran prairie endurance now sits, quietly, inside three children in Grande Prairie alongside the Yorkshire, the Hawaiian, the Norwegian, the Dutch, the Irish, and the Hebridean.</p><p>Bette outlived Sam by ten years. She died at <strong>Grande Prairie on 19 January 2025</strong>, aged eighty-two &mdash; recent enough that she is not a name in an archive but <strong>Nana Bette</strong>, remembered, photographed holding each of her great-grandchildren in turn.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-where-the-steinke-line-joins-the-others"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">The Living Line</span>Where the Steinke line joins the others</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Grande Prairie · the confluence</p><p>Every family line in this larger story ends the same way &mdash; not at a single heir, but at a confluence. The Steinke line is the youngest-known and the shortest-traced of them, but it arrives at the same place as all the rest.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>That is what durchhalten was for.</p></aside><p>It runs through <strong>Bette Doreen Steinke</strong> to her daughter <strong>Lana Lyn Rycroft</strong> (1961&ndash;2015), and through Lana to her daughter <strong>Melanie Lyn Haiste</strong> (born 1983), and through Melanie &mdash; with <strong>Thomas Cheesman</strong>, the co-parents of three children &mdash; to <strong>Patience, Daniel, and Faith</strong>, raised in Grande Prairie. They inherit the Steinke share of themselves together with everything else: the Rycroft reach, the Haiste coming-through, the Lakeman <em>doorkomen</em>, the Docherty thole, the Hebridean <em>buanseasmhachd</em> &mdash; and, from Henry and Martha&rsquo;s prairie, <em>durchhalten</em>, the holding-out.</p><p>None of it is finished. The Steinke story, in particular, is barely begun &mdash; its European half still waiting in records nobody in this family has yet read. But the holding has already done its work: it brought a German Lutheran family across an ocean and a continent, held a corner of the Peace, and handed three children a name and a grit they will carry whether or not they ever learn the village it came from.</p><p>That is what <em>durchhalten</em> was for.</p><details class="heritage-longread__notes">
<summary>Notes on the records</summary>
<div class="heritage-longread__notes-body">
<p>This is the <strong>thinnest-traced</strong> of the family lines, and the page says so plainly rather than dressing the gap.</p><p><strong>What is verified.</strong> <strong>Bette Doreen Steinke&rsquo;s parents were Henry and Martha Steinke</strong>, and Bette was their fourth child &mdash; established by her own 2025 obituary (Bear Creek Funeral Home, Grande Prairie), a family-supplied primary source. Her vital dates (b. 26 April 1942 Sexsmith; m. Sam Rycroft 30 October 1959 Sexsmith; d. 19 January 2025 Grande Prairie) and her children (Lorne, Lana, Vance, Clark) are firm.</p><p><strong>What is open.</strong> Everything before Henry and Martha. Their own birthplaces and birth years, their parents, their year and port of immigration, the country and village they came from (Germany proper? a German colony in Russia &mdash; Volga, Black Sea, Volhynia? Prussia or Pomerania?), and whether they made a southern-prairie stop before the Peace &mdash; none of it is yet confirmed. A Steinke cluster at the <strong>Emmaus Lutheran Cemetery, Teepee Creek</strong>, points toward a <strong>Volhynian-German / East-Prussian Lutheran</strong> origin, but that link has <em>not</em> been verified and is not asserted here as fact.</p><p><strong>The next records to pull</strong>, in order of value: Bette&rsquo;s <strong>1959 Sexsmith marriage record</strong> (it should name her parents outright); the <strong>1931 Canadian census</strong> (released 2023) and the 1921/1911 censuses, for a Henry/Heinrich Steinke household near Sexsmith &mdash; or farther south, if they were second-stage migrants; the <strong>immigration manifest</strong> at Library and Archives Canada; and a direct surname search of the Peace Country local histories &mdash; <strong><em>Wagon Trails Grown Over: Sexsmith to the Smoky</em></strong> and <strong><em>Pioneers of the Peace</em></strong> &mdash; at the South Peace Regional Archives, where a single family-submitted Steinke entry could give names, dates, and a village in one stroke.</p><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>This is a working page, honest about being mostly a beginning. It will grow as the censuses and the German parish records give up what they hold. Until then it asserts only what a primary source supports &mdash; and names the rest as the work still to do.</em></p>
